Z||000000||blue87SE||04-11-2001||12:59 AM||||If anyone can help me with this I would appreciate it... I want to add cruise to my SE and I need to know what all I would need and how hard it would be to do and if it would work...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||rogergarrison||04-11-2001||07:03 AM||||If you do, I think youll have to use an OEM setup. Would be a lot of parts, wiring. Aftermarket usually operate from magnets mounted on driveshaft--and Fieros dont have one. Its been a long time since I checked though, may be different now. With OEM you have to have the cruise control computer, the vacum motor with wiring/vacum harness, new turn signal/stalk, wiring harness to go to brake and clutch pedals, wiring harness to connect to tach and I dont know what other stuff too.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Carrolles||04-11-2001||07:29 AM||||I purchased an OEM look-alike from JC Whitney.  It uses input from the VSS signal instead of magnets strapped to the driveshaft.<P>I haven't gotten around to installing it yet but it seems pretty straightforward.  The turn signal stalk looks just like the original.  Wiring picks up signals from the VSS (splice into the harness at the ecm), the brake switch near the brake pedal, tach signal (wiring harness at the ecm), and power and ground.  It does not connect to the neutral switch or clutch lock out switch.  Instead it monitors the tach and speed sensor.  If you slip it into neutral or push in the clutch is senses the quick rise in rpms and disengages.<P>The servo mounts to the original mounting bracket in the engine bay (may need a little modification to work) and the cable hooks to the throttle body via a large assortment of clips.  You pick up vacuum from a connector near the servo mounting bracket.  The manual seems to be fairly complete.<P>When I finally get around to installing mine I'll post pictures of what is involved.<P>If I remember correctly I paid around $130.00 US.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
